                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Marble Duel is mess of a game that should've stayed on mobile platforms, or at least, been properly rebalanced for PC gaming.\n\nBefore I launch into everything that's bad about this game (and there's LOTS to say about that!), let me give you some background on Marble Duel.\n\nOriginally, the game was called Evy: Magic Spheres and sold on mobile platforms, as well as Desura. When coming to Steam, the name was changed to Marble Duel. Personally, I think the old name sounds better, as the current title reminds me of Marble Madness, which is nothing like this game at all.\n\nMarble Duel looks similar to games like Luxor or Zuma, but it plays very differently. You need to match three or more colors to do something (e.g. damage the enemy, raise shields on yourself, heal yourself, or increase your attack power), but unlike those two other games, Marble Duel is turn-based as opposed to real-time action.\n\nThe goal is simple: kill your opponent before they kill you. During the game, you'll be able to increase your skills, which directly translates to differently colored marbles. You can respec at any time, so if you put points into something you don't need much, it's easy to fix.   \n\nThe graphics are entirely 2D and the artwork for the cutscenes are passable. The audio is okay, but I prefer it turned off so I can play my own music. \n\nThe story is utterly forgettable and more a nuisance than anything else. In this sort of puzzle game, I'm far less interested in the storyline (especially when it's written so poorly) than I am in actual gameplay.\n\nNow the bad stuff... yeah, what I wrote up above is what I'd consider the \"good\" part of Marble Duel.\n\nThe developer KNOWINGLY placed false screenshots on the store. His response to this when someone on the boards called him out on it was this: [url=http://steamcommunity.com/app/338120/discussions/0/490125737463480720/#c490125737465283879]\"Well, t'would be awfully hard to explain what's actually going on in the game without those graphic additions.\"[/url]\n\nIn other words, the developer doesn't really care that he use false imagery to sell his product. \n\nBecause Marble Duel was designed with mobile games in mind, the game was balanced for in-app purchases. Fortunately, the developer removed those before putting it on Steam, but in the process, failed to rebalance the game as well. That means the game's difficulty is such that it expects you to purchase gems or coins (or whatever stupid in-game currency was used in the mobile version) and without that feature, Marble Duel quickly becomes so difficult that it's impossible to play.\n\nHere are three examples:\n\n1. When playing the game, the line of marbles extends off the screen and wraps around elsewhere. The AI can apparently \"see\" what's off screen and can make moves based on that knowledge. The player, however, cannot see off screen and is therefore at a disadvantage. \n\n2. Which marbles you get to shoot and match is randomized. That's supposed to be part of the strategy in learning to make moves with future moves in-mind. Unfortunately, the AI will ALWAYS receive whatever marbles it needs to make a move. If the AI needs a red marble or a green marble, it will get them, whereas the player will have to rely on pure chance.\n\n3. I've played one stage over 60 times (and lost each time) where if I win, I get a new amulet with bonus health. The problem is the AI starts with huge advantages over me. The AI has 400 HP, and can make THREE turns each round. In contrast, the highest my health can be right now (with all available points redistributed to my health stat) is 80 HP, and each round I get TWO turns. \n\nAll in all, I'd strongly suggest staying away from Marble Duel. If I could get a refund, I would, but I've played more than two hours worth of the game. \n\nIf you're desperate for a puzzle game, go elsewhere, as there are tons of them on Steam. If you're deadset on getting Marble Duel, wait for a sale. I know the normal price is $4.99 USD, but I'd seriously recommend holding off until the price is $1 or less. Even then, the frustration you'll feel when playing may not be worth it.",
